; EXERCISE 3: SIBLINGS, AUNTS, A FAMILY
;
; The family tree of theory/family.kurt. Replace each `todo` by a proof; don't change the `show`
; lines. Check with `kurt 3-siblings.kurt`.
;
; Two people are siblings if they are different and have a common parent ("sibling"). Read
; `x ≠ y` as `¬(x = y)`: equality.kurt has the rules "not-equal-intro" and "not-equal-elim" to
; go from one to the other, and `≠` is symmetric, like `=`.

load theory/family
const Anna, Ben, Clara, David, Eva

; A family: Anna is a parent of Clara and David, Clara is a parent of Eva, David of Ben. Each
; part says which of these facts it needs, on the left of its `⇒`.
;
;           Anna
;          /    \
;      Clara    David
;        |        |
;       Eva      Ben

; (a) Anna is a grandparent of Eva.
show parent(Anna, Clara) ∧ parent(Clara, Eva) ⇒ grandparent(Anna, Eva)
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) Clara is an aunt of Ben, the child of her sibling David.
;     Hint: first prove `sibling(Clara, David)`, step by step.
show female(Clara) ∧ parent(Anna, Clara) ∧ parent(Anna, David) ∧ Clara ≠ David ∧ parent(David, Ben) ⇒ aunt(Clara, Ben)
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) Nobody is their own sibling.
show ∀ x ¬sibling(x, x)
proof
    todo
qed

; (d) Being siblings is symmetric.
show ∀ x ∀ y (sibling(x, y) ⇒ sibling(y, x))
proof
    todo
qed

; (e) Two siblings have a common grandparent.
;     Hint: `pick` their common parent p. Who is a parent of p? (Inside the block, don't call
;     the new person `g`: `g` is bound in the `∃ g` you want to prove. Call it `q`.)
show ∀ x ∀ y (sibling(x, y) ⇒ ∃ g (grandparent(g, x) ∧ grandparent(g, y)))
proof
    todo
qed

; (f) An only child: if David has no sibling, then every child of Anna is David.
;     Hint: `let x`, `assume parent(Anna, x)`. To prove `x = David`, assume `¬(x = David)` and
;     find a sibling of David.
show parent(Anna, David) ∧ ¬∃ y sibling(David, y) ⇒ ∀ x (parent(Anna, x) ⇒ x = David)
proof
    todo
qed
